## Understanding the Mutation

The mutation is on line 74:
- Original: `static_cast<float>(result)`
- Mutated: `result` (cast removed)

Let me look at the context:

```cpp
template<typename scalar_t, int VEC_SIZE>
__global__ void softplus_vectorized_kernel(...) {
    ...
    if (idx + VEC_SIZE <= size) {
        ...
        for (int i = 0; i < VEC_SIZE; i++) {
            scalar_t val;
            if constexpr (std::is_same<scalar_t, half>::value && VEC_SIZE == 8) {
                half* half_ptr = reinterpret_cast<half*>(&vec_in);
                val = half_ptr[i];
            } else {
                float* float_ptr = reinterpret_cast<float*>(&vec_in);
                val = static_cast<scalar_t>(float_ptr[i]);  // Line ~62
            }

            scalar_t result = softplus_activation<scalar_t, VEC_SIZE>(val, beta);

            if constexpr (std::is_same<scalar_t, half>::value && VEC_SIZE == 8) {
                half* half_out = reinterpret_cast<half*>(&vec_out);
                half_out[i] = result;
            } else {
                float* float_out = reinterpret_cast<float*>(&vec_out);
                float_out[i] = static_cast<float>(result);  // Line 74 - ORIGINAL
                // MUTANT: float_out[i] = result;
            }
        }
        ...
    }
}
```

The input specification says:
- Shape: [16, 16384]
- dtype: torch.float32


- `scalar_t = float` and `VEC_SIZE = 4` for this configuration
- `result` is already type `float` from the softplus computation

Since `result` is already `float` and `float_out` points to `float` data, the explicit cast to `float` is redundant. The mutation simply removes this unnecessary type conversion, which has no functional impact on the assignment.

For the kernel execution with float32 input, the else branch executes because the condition `(std::is_same<scalar_t, half>::value && VEC_SIZE == 8)` evaluates to false—neither the half-precision nor the specific vector size requirement is met. With 262144 total elements and a block size of 256, the grid spans 256 blocks, ensuring all elements reach the else branch where this mutation occurs.

The cast itself produces no observable difference since `result` is already float type, and casting float to float is semantically identical. Both the original and mutated versions assign the same value to the output array. This represents a true equivalent mutation rather than a coverage gap—the redundant cast doesn't alter program behavior in any measurable way. unkillable because the cast operation has no practical effect when the input dtype is float32—the value is already in the correct type, making the mutation semantically identical to the original code.
